 Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    2.  [A.2 Classifier ff details](#A1.SS2 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    3.  [A.3 Choosing number of components KK](#A1.SS3 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    4.  [A.4 Other hyperparameters and ablation studies](#A1.SS4 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    5.  [A.5 Further discussion on Interpretations](#A1.SS5 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n        1.  [A.5.1 Corruption samples ESC-50](#A1.SS5.SSS1 \"In A.5 Further discussion on Interpretations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n        2.  [A.5.2 Misclassification samples ESC-50](#A1.SS5.SSS2 \"In A.5 Further discussion on Interpretations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n        3.  [A.5.3 Coherence in interpretations](#A1.SS5.SSS3 \"In A.5 Further discussion on Interpretations ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    6.  [A.6 Discussion on interpretations from related methods](#A1.SS6 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n        1.  [A.6.1 Attribution maps for listenable output](#A1.SS6.SSS1 \"In A.6 Discussion on interpretations from related methods ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n        2.  [A.6.2 Interpretations of FLINT](#A1.SS6.SSS2 \"In A.6 Discussion on interpretations from related methods ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    7.  [A.7 Baseline implementations details](#A1.SS7 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    8.  [A.8 Subjective evaluation implementation](#A1.SS8 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n    9.  [A.9 Potential Societal Impacts](#A1.SS9 \"In Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")\n\n[License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:2202.11479v2 \\[cs.SD\\] 24 Oct 2022\n\nListen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\n==========================================================================\n\nJayneel Parekh Affiliation: LTCI, Télécom Paris, Institut Polytechnique de Paris Email: [<redacted>](mailto:)    Sanjeel Parekh ††thanks: Work conducted while the author was at Télécom Paris Affiliation: LTCI, Télécom Paris, Institut Polytechnique de Paris Affiliation: Audio Analytic Email: [<redacted>](mailto:)    Pavlo Mozharovskyi Affiliation: LTCI, Télécom Paris, Institut Polytechnique de Paris Email: [<redacted>](mailto:)    Florence d’Alché-Buc Affiliation: LTCI, Télécom Paris, Institut Polytechnique de Paris Email: [<redacted>](mailto:)    Gaël Richard Affiliation: LTCI, Télécom Paris, Institut Polytechnique de Paris Email: [<redacted>](mailto:)\n\n###### Abstract\n\nThis paper tackles post-hoc interpretability for audio processing networks. Our goal is to interpret decisions of a trained network in terms of high-level audio objects that are also listenable for the end-user. To this end, we propose a novel interpreter design that incorporates non-negative matrix factorization (NMF). In particular, a regularized interpreter module is trained to take hidden layer representations of the targeted network as input and produce time activations of pre-learnt NMF components as intermediate outputs. Our methodology allows us to generate intuitive audio-based interpretations that explicitly enhance parts of the input signal most relevant for a network’s decision. We demonstrate our method’s applicability on popular benchmarks, including a real-world multi-label classification task.\n\n1 Introduction\n--------------\n\nDeep learning models, while state-of-the-art for several tasks in domains such as computer vision, natural language processing and audio, are typically not interpretable. Their increasing use, especially in decision-critical domains, necessitates interpreting their decisions. A good interpretation is often characterized by its understandability for the end users (see for instance \\[[16](#bib.bib16)\\]). More importantly, attributes that aid understandability may largely be dependent on the data modality. In this paper, our aim is to generate post-hoc human–understandable interpretations for deep networks that process the audio modality. Here, post-hoc interpretability refers to the problem of interpreting decisions of a fixed pre–trained network.\n\nTraditional approaches generate interpretations through input attribution, either directly on the raw input features or on a given simplified representation \\[[32](#bib.bib32), [42](#bib.bib42), [38](#bib.bib38), [28](#bib.bib28)\\]. To generate more understandable interpretations, a small number of approaches consider other means, such as logical rules \\[[39](#bib.bib39)\\], sentences \\[[19](#bib.bib19)\\] and high-level concepts \\[[15](#bib.bib15)\\].\n\nMost existing post-hoc interpretability methods are primarily designed for application to images and tabular data. This limits their applicability to other data modalities such as audio. Although many audio processing networks operate on spectrogram-like representations, which can be seen as 2D time-frequency images, a visualization or attribution in this space is not as meaningful to a common user as it is for images \\[[27](#bib.bib27)\\].\n\nThis leads us to build an interpretation system that takes into account audio–specific understandability features. We motivate these features through an example: suppose an audio event detection network deployed in a house recognizes an “alarm” sound event. An ideal interpreter for this classification decision would have the ability to “show” that it was indeed an alarm sound that triggered this decision. To do so, it must be able to localize the alarm amid other events in the house (for e.g. dog barks, baby cries, background noise etc.) and make it listenable for the end–user. It is important to highlight here the role of listenable interpretations for better understanding of an audio network’s decisions – note that it would be much less meaningful for a human to see the alarm sound as highlighted parts of a spectrogram. Thus making the following aspects important for our system design: (i) generating interpretations in terms of high–level audio objects that constitute a scene, (ii) segmenting parts of the input signal most relevant for a decision and providing it as listenable audio. It’s worth emphasizing that audio interpretability is not the same as classical tasks of separation or denoising. These tasks involve recovering complete object of interest in the output audio. On the other hand, a classifier network might focus more on salient regions. When interpreting its decision and making it listenable we expect to uncover such regions and not necessarily the complete object of interest.\n\nTo this end, we propose a novel post–hoc interpreter for audio that employs a popular signal decomposition technique called Non–negative Matrix Factorization (NMF; [Lee and Seung \\[26\\]](#bib.bib26)). NMF seeks to decompose an audio signal into constituent spectral patterns and their temporal activations. Unlike principal component analysis, NMF is known to provide part–based decompositions \\[[12](#bib.bib12)\\]. Owing to these properties, we first use NMF to pre-learn a spectral pattern dictionary on our training data. This dictionary is then incorporated as a fixed decoder within our interpretation module. Specifically, we train our system to determine an intermediate encoding that performs two roles: (i) is able to reconstruct the input through the fixed NMF dictionary decoder, thus corresponding to time activations for dictionary components, (ii) at the same time, a function of this encoding is able to mimic the classifier’s output. Training with these constraints allows us to generate, for any classifier decision, importance values over spectral patterns in our dictionary. Listenable interpretations are readily produced by inverting most important NMF spectral patterns back to the time domain.\n\nIn summary, we make the following contributions:\n\n*   •\n    \n    We propose a novel NMF-based interpreter module for post-hoc interpretability that generates interpretations in terms of meaningful high–level audio objects, listenable for the end–user.\n    \n*   •\n    \n    We present an original formulation that constrains the interpreter encoding through two loss functions, one for input reconstruction through NMF dictionary and the other for fidelity to the network’s decision. From a learning perspective, we show a new way to link NMF with deep neural networks, especially for generating interpretations.\n    \n*   •\n    \n    We extensively evaluate on two popular audio event analysis benchmarks, tackling both multi–class and multi–label classification tasks. The dataset for the latter is very challenging due to its collection in noisy real–world setting. Our method’s design allows us to simulate feature removal and perform faithfulness evaluation.\n    \n\n2 Related Works\n---------------\n\nIn this section, we position our work in relation to: (i) interpretability methods for audio, (ii) methods for concept–based interpretability and, (iii) use of NMF within the audio community, in particular, attempts to link it with deep networks.\n\nInterpretability methods for audio Some approaches \\[[5](#bib.bib5), [50](#bib.bib50)\\] have shown usability of attention/visualization techniques for interpreting audio processing networks or generated instance-wise feature importance \\[[52](#bib.bib52)\\] for time-series data \\[[46](#bib.bib46)\\]. However, our focus is on methods that attempt to address audio interpretability beyond image-based visualizations or raw input attribution. Muckenhirn et al. \\[[33](#bib.bib33)\\] illustrate usefulness of GuidedBackprop \\[[44](#bib.bib44)\\] for analyzing CNNs operating on raw 1D waveforms by analyzing relevance signal in frequency domain. This analysis does not extend to spectrogram input or address listenability of interpretations. A few works have applied the popular LIME algorithm with a simplified input representation more suited for audio. In particular, SLIME \\[[30](#bib.bib30), [31](#bib.bib31)\\] proposes to segment the input along time or frequency. The input is perturbed by switching \"on/off\" the individual segments. AudioLIME \\[[18](#bib.bib18), [10](#bib.bib10)\\] proposes to separate input using predefined sources to create the simplified representation. AudioLIME arguably generates more meaningful interpretations than SLIME as it relies on audio objects readily listenable for end-user. However, it can only be applied for limited applications as it requires existence of known and meaningful predefined sources that compose the input audio. APNet \\[[53](#bib.bib53)\\] takes another promising direction by extending interpretable prototypical networks for audio input. However, they propose an interpretable system by-design. They don’t tackle the problem of post-hoc interpretation.\n\nConcept-based interpretability Our method relies on high-level objects for interpretation. In this sense, it is most closely related to post–hoc concept-based methods \\[[21](#bib.bib21), [15](#bib.bib15)\\]. An interesting approach is that of post-hoc version of FLINT \\[[34](#bib.bib34)\\] with whom we share the idea of utilizing the hidden layers and loss functions to encourage interpretability. However we crucially differ from FLINT and other related approaches in concept representation and their applicability for audio interpretations. FLINT represents concepts by a dictionary of attribute functions over input space. The learnt concepts are not obviously comprehensible to a user, requiring a separate visualization pipeline to get insights. Approaches based on TCAV \\[[21](#bib.bib21)\\], such as ACE \\[[15](#bib.bib15)\\], ConceptSHAP \\[[51](#bib.bib51)\\], define concept using a set of images and learn a representation for it in terms of hidden layers of the network, termed as concept activation vector (CAV). These designs for concepts are not related to our NMF-inspired dictionary representation. Importantly, none of the above mentioned approaches can generate listenable interpretations which is key for understandability of audio processing networks.\n\nNMF for audio has been widely used for numerous tasks ranging from separation to transcription \\[[43](#bib.bib43), [48](#bib.bib48), [11](#bib.bib11), [6](#bib.bib6)\\]. Its traditional usage as a supervised dictionary or feature learning method involves learning class-wise dictionaries over training data \\[[12](#bib.bib12)\\]. Time activations, which are the so-called features, are generated for any data point by projecting it onto the learnt dictionaries. Extracted features can subsequently be used for downstream tasks such as classification. [Bisot et al. \\[7\\]](#bib.bib7) couple NMF-based features with neural networks to boost performance of acoustic scene classification.\n\nNMF has also been successfully employed with audio–visual deep learning models for separation \\[[13](#bib.bib13)\\] and classification \\[[35](#bib.bib35)\\]. Another line of research explored unfolding iterations of different NMF optimization algorithms as a deep neural network \\[[24](#bib.bib24), [49](#bib.bib49)\\]. These systems, commonly known as Deep NMF, have primarily been used for audio source separation tasks.\n\nWhile these works share with us the idea of combining neural networks and NMF, there is no overlap between our goals and methodologies. Unlike aforementioned studies, we wish to investigate a classifier’s decision in a post-hoc manner using NMF as a regularizer. Furthermore, to our best knowledge, attempting to regress temporal activations of a fixed NMF dictionary by accessing intermediate layers of an audio classification network is novel even within the NMF literature.\n\n3 System Design\n---------------\n\n![Refer to caption](2202.11479v2/figures/audio_fig_v3.png)\n\nFigure 1: System overview: The interpretation module (right block) accesses hidden layer outputs of the network being interpreted (left block). These are used to predict an intermediate encoding. Through regularization terms, we encourage this encoding to both mimic the classifier’s output and also serve as the time activations of a pre-learnt NMF dictionary.\n\nWe begin this section with a brief note on data notation and NMF. Subsequently, in Sec. [3.1](#S3.SS1 \"3.1 Interpreter Design ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\"), we discuss interpreter module’s design and learning. This is followed by a description of our interpretation generation methodology in Sec. [3.2](#S3.SS2 \"3.2 Interpretation generation ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\"). An overview of the proposed system is presented in Fig. [1](#S3.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\").\n\nData notation.  We denote a training dataset by 𝒮:=(x,y)i\\=1N\\\\mathcal{S}:=(x,y)\\_{i=1}^{N}, where xx is the time domain audio signal and yy, a label vector. The label vector could be a one-hot or binary encoding depending upon a multi-class or multi-label dataset, respectively. Since multiple audio representations are used in this paper, a note on their notation is in order. Very often, audio signals are processed in the frequency domain through a short-time Fourier transform (STFT) on xx called spectrogram. Log-mel spectrograms are a popular input to audio classification networks \\[[36](#bib.bib36)\\], which is also the one we use in this paper. To keep notation simple, we refer to input of the network with xx. For NMF however, we favor a representation of xx that can be easily inverted back to the time-domain and use a log–magnitude spectrogram 𝐗\\\\mathbf{X} that is computed by applying an element-wise transformation x0→log(1+x0)x\\_{0}\\\\rightarrow\\\\log(1+x\\_{0}) on the magnitude spectrogram. This is preferred over using magnitude spectrograms as it corresponds more closely to human perception of sound intensity \\[[17](#bib.bib17)\\].\n\nNMF basics.  NMF is a popular technique for unsupervised decomposition of audio signals \\[[3](#bib.bib3)\\]. Given any positive time–frequency representation 𝐗∈ℝ+F×T{\\\\mathbf{X}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{F\\\\times T}\\_{+} consisting of FF frequency bins and TT time frames, NMF decomposes it into a product of two non-negative matrices, such that,\n\n𝐗≈𝐖𝐇{\\\\mathbf{X}}\\\\approx{\\\\mathbf{W}}{\\\\mathbf{H}}\n\nHere, 𝐖\\=\\[𝐰1,𝐰2,…,𝐰K\\]∈ℝ+F×K{\\\\mathbf{W}}=\\[{\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{1}},{\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{2}},\\\\ldots,{\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{K}}\\]\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{F\\\\times K}\\_{+} is interpreted as the spectral pattern or dictionary matrix containing KK components and 𝐇\\=\\[𝐡1,𝐡2,…,𝐡K\\]⊺∈ℝ+K×T{\\\\mathbf{H}}=\\[{\\\\mathbf{h}\\_{1}},{\\\\mathbf{h}\\_{2}},\\\\ldots,{\\\\mathbf{h}\\_{K}}\\]^{\\\\intercal}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{K\\\\times T}\\_{+} a matrix containing the corresponding time activations. Typically, a β\\\\beta\\-divergence measure between 𝐗{\\\\mathbf{X}} and 𝐖𝐇{\\\\mathbf{W}}{\\\\mathbf{H}} is minimized and multiplicative updates are used for estimating 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}} and 𝐇{\\\\mathbf{H}} \\[[26](#bib.bib26)\\]. Note that it is possible to reconstruct signal corresponding to each or a group of spectral components. This is typically done using a procedure called soft–masking. For a single component kk, this is written as,\n\n𝐗k\\=𝐰k𝐡k⊺𝐖𝐇⊙𝐗\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{k}=\\\\frac{{\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{k}}{\\\\mathbf{h}\\_{k}}^{\\\\intercal}}{{\\\\mathbf{W}}{\\\\mathbf{H}}}\\\\odot\\\\mathbf{X}\n\nBoth ./../. and ⊙\\\\odot are element-wise operations. If 𝐗\\\\mathbf{X} is an invertible representation of the magnitude spectrogram, time domain signal for 𝐗k\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{k} is easily recovered using the inverse STFT operation. We extensively utilize this procedure for generating listenable interpretations. NMF can also be used for dictionary learning, by estimating 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}} on a training dataset matrix 𝐗train{\\\\mathbf{X}}\\_{\\\\text{train}}. As discussed later, we use a variant of NMF called Sparse-NMF \\[[25](#bib.bib25)\\] to pre-learn dictionary for subsequent usage in the interpretation module.\n\n### 3.1 Interpreter Design\n\nAs depicted in Fig. [1](#S3.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\") the interpreter module ℐ\\\\mathcal{I} contains two components: an interpreter network and a NMF dictionary decoder. The so-called interpreter network computes the following function x↦Θ∘Ψ∘fℐ(x)x\\\\mapsto\\\\Theta\\\\circ\\\\Psi\\\\circ f\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x) where Ψ\\\\Psi is the function responsible for generating an intermediate encoding from hidden layer representations of the classification network, and Θ\\\\Theta attempts to mimic the classifier’s output given the intermediate representation. The NMF decoder based on a pre-trained dictionary plays two roles: (i) during training, it constrains the intermediate representation to correspond to time activations of a pre-learnt spectral pattern dictionary and (ii) when interpreting a classifier’s prediction, it is used to build listenable interpretation. To the best of our knowledge, this is an original usage of NMF that allows us to interpret a network’s decisions in terms of a fixed dictionary.\n\nDesign of Ψ\\\\Psi.  The function Ψ\\\\Psi processes outputs of a set of hidden layers of the classifier, given by fℐ(x)f\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x). It’s output, Ψ(fℐ(x))∈ℝ+K×T\\\\Psi(f\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x))\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}\\_{+}^{K\\\\times T} produces an intermediate encoding of the interpreter. For simplicity, we will denote this intermediate encoding as 𝐇ℐ(x)\\=Ψ∘fℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)=\\\\Psi\\\\circ f\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x), a function over input xx.\n\nIn practice, Ψ\\\\Psi is modelled as a neural network that takes as input convolutional feature maps from different layers of ff. To concatenate and perform joint processing on them, each feature map is first appropriately transformed to ensure same width and height dimensions. Two important aspects were kept in mind while designing subsequent layers of Ψ\\\\Psi. Firstly, audio feature maps for spectrogram-like inputs naturally contain the notion of time and frequency along the width and height dimensions. Secondly, through appropriate regularization we wish to produce an intermediate encoding that also serves as time activations of the pre-learnt NMF dictionary, a matrix of dimensions K×TK\\\\times T. To achieve this, we continuously downsample on the frequency axis and upsample the time axis to TT frames. Similarly, the number of input feature maps is re-sampled to reach a size of KK, equal to the number of components in dictionary 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}}. Additionally, we ensure non-negativity of 𝐇ℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x) through use of ReLU activation. All learnable parameters of Ψ\\\\Psi are denoted by VΨV\\_{\\\\Psi}.\n\nDesign of Θ\\\\Theta.  𝐇ℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x), the intermediate encoding output by Ψ\\\\Psi is then fed to Θ\\\\Theta, which aims to mimic output of the classifier. This directly helps in learning a representation which can interpret f(x)f(x). Its design consists of two parts. The first part pools activations 𝐇ℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x) across time. While this pooling can be implemented in multiple ways, we opt for attention–based pooling \\[[20](#bib.bib20)\\], i.e., 𝐳\\=𝐇ℐ(x)𝐚\\\\mathbf{z}={\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)\\\\mathbf{a}, where 𝐚∈ℝT\\\\mathbf{a}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{T} are the attention weights and 𝐳∈ℝK\\\\mathbf{z}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{K} is the pooled vector. The pooled representation vector is passed through a linear layer. This is followed by an appropriate activation function to convert its output to probabilities, that is, softmax for multi-class classification and sigmoïd for multi-label classification. All learnable parameters of Θ\\\\Theta are denoted by VΘV\\_{\\\\Theta}.\n\nFidelity loss.  Generalized cross–entropy between interpreter’s output Θ(𝐇ℐ(x))\\\\Theta({\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)) and classifier’s output f(x)f(x) is minimized to encourage interpreter to mimic the classifier. For multi-class classification this loss function is written as,\n\nℒFID(x,VΨ,VΘ)\\=−f(x)⊺log(Θ(𝐇ℐ(x)))\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{FID}}(x,V\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta})=-f(x)^{\\\\intercal}\\\\log(\\\\Theta({\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)))\n\n(1)\n\nOn the other hand, for multi-label classification this loss reads,\n\nℒFID(x,VΨ,VΘ)\\=−∑f(x)⊙log(Θ(𝐇ℐ(x)))+(1−f(x))⊙log(1−Θ(𝐇ℐ(x))).\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{FID}}(x,V\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta})=-\\\\sum f(x)\\\\odot\\\\log(\\\\Theta({\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)))+(1-f(x))\\\\odot\\\\log(1-\\\\Theta({\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x))).\n\n(2)\n\nHere ⊙\\\\odot denotes element-wise multiplication.\n\nNMF dictionary decoder and regularization.  We additionally constrain the intermediate encoding, such that, when fed to a decoder it is able to reconstruct the input audio. As already discussed, we choose this decoder to be a pre-learnt NMF dictionary, 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}}. Formally, through ℒNMF\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{NMF}} we require 𝐇ℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x) to approximate log-magnitude spectrogram 𝐗\\\\mathbf{X} of input audio xx as 𝐗≈𝐖𝐇ℐ(x)\\\\mathbf{X}\\\\approx{\\\\mathbf{W}}{\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x):\n\nℒNMF(x,VΨ)\\=‖𝐗−𝐖𝐇ℐ(x)‖22.\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{NMF}}(x,V\\_{\\\\Psi})=\\\\|\\\\mathbf{X}-{\\\\mathbf{W}}{\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)\\\\|^{2}\\_{2}.\n\n(3)\n\nThis allows us to consider 𝐇ℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x) as a time activation matrix for 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}}.\n\nTraining loss.  In addition to ℒFID\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{FID}} and ℒNMF\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{NMF}}, we impose ℓ1\\\\ell\\_{1} regularization on 𝐇ℐ(x){\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x) to encourage sparsity and well-behavedness, as is often done in classical NMF \\[[25](#bib.bib25)\\]. The complete training loss function over our training dataset 𝒮\\\\mathcal{S} can thus be given as:\n\nℒ(VΨ,VΘ)\\=∑x∈𝒮ℒFID(x,VΨ,VΘ)+αℒNMF(x,VΨ)+β‖𝐇ℐ(x)‖1\\\\mathcal{L}(V\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta})=\\\\sum\\_{x\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{FID}}(x,V\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta})+\\\\alpha\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{NMF}}(x,V\\_{\\\\Psi})+\\\\beta||{\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)||\\_{1}\n\n(4)\n\nwhere α,β≥0\\\\alpha,\\\\beta\\\\geq 0 are loss hyperparameters. All the parameters of the system are constituted in the functions Ψ,Θ\\\\Psi,\\\\Theta and dictionary 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}}. Since 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}} is pre-learnt and fixed, the training loss ℒ\\\\mathcal{L} is optimized only w.r.t VΨ,VΘV\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta}. As a reminder, when training the interpreter for post-hoc analysis, the classifier network is kept fixed.\n\nAlgorithm 1 Learning algorithm\n\n1:  Input: Classifier ff, Training data 𝒮\\\\mathcal{S}, parameters V\\={VΨ,VΘ}V=\\\\{V\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta}\\\\}, hyperparameters {α,β,μ}\\\\{\\\\alpha,\\\\beta,\\\\mu\\\\}, number of batches BB, number of training epochs NepochN\\_{\\\\text{epoch}}\n\n2:  𝐖←{\\\\mathbf{W}}\\\\leftarrow pre-learn NMF dictionary (𝒮,μ\\\\mathcal{S},\\\\mu) {// Sparse-NMF algorithm}\n\n3:  Random initialization of parameters V0V\\_{0}\n\n4:  V^←\\\\hat{V}\\\\leftarrow train (f,𝒮,𝐖,V0,α,β,B,Nepochf,\\\\mathcal{S},{\\\\mathbf{W}},V\\_{0},\\\\alpha,\\\\beta,B,N\\_{\\\\text{epoch}}) {// Train with ℒ\\\\mathcal{L} in Eq. [4](#S3.E4 \"Equation 4 ‣ 3.1 Interpreter Design ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")}\n\n5:  Output: V^\\={V^Ψ,V^Θ}\\\\hat{V}=\\\\{\\\\hat{V}\\_{\\\\Psi},\\\\hat{V}\\_{\\\\Theta}\\\\}\n\nLearning algorithm.  The complete learning pipeline is presented in Algorithm [1](#alg1 \"Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 Interpreter Design ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\"). The learnable parameters of the interpreter module are given by V\\={VΨ,VΘ}V=\\\\{V\\_{\\\\Psi},V\\_{\\\\Theta}\\\\}. The pre-specified dictionary (Step 2 in Algorithm [1](#alg1 \"Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.1 Interpreter Design ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")) is learnt using Sparse-NMF \\[[25](#bib.bib25)\\] wherein, the following optimization problem is solved through multiplicative updates to pre-learn 𝐖{\\\\mathbf{W}}:\n\nminD(𝐗train|𝐖𝐇)+μ∥𝐇∥1s.t.𝐖≥0,𝐇≥0,∥𝐰k∥\\=1,∀k.\\\\min D({\\\\mathbf{X}}\\_{\\\\text{train}}|{\\\\mathbf{W}}{\\\\mathbf{H}})+\\\\mu\\\\|{\\\\mathbf{H}}\\\\|\\_{1}\\\\quad s.t.{\\\\mathbf{W}}\\\\geq 0,{\\\\mathbf{H}}\\\\geq 0,\\\\|\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{k}\\\\|=1,~\\\\forall k.\n\n(5)\n\nHere D(.|.)D(.|.) is a divergence cost function. In practice, euclidean distance is used. The reader is referred to appendix [A.1](#A1.SS1 \"A.1 Sparse-NMF implementation details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\") for more details regarding Sparse-NMF optimization problem, such as construction of 𝐗train{\\\\mathbf{X}}\\_{\\\\text{train}} on our datasets.\n\n### 3.2 Interpretation generation\n\nFinally, to generate audio that interprets the classifier’s decision for a sample xx and a predicted class cc, we follow a two-step procedure: The first step consists of selecting the components which are considered “important\" for the prediction. This is determined by estimating their relevance using the pooled time activations in Θ\\\\Theta and the weights for linear layer, and then thresholding it. Precisely, given a sample xx, the pooled activations are computed as 𝐳\\=𝐇ℐ(x)𝐚\\\\mathbf{z}={\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)\\\\mathbf{a}. Denoting the weights for class cc in the linear layer as θcw\\\\theta\\_{c}^{w}, the relevance of component kk is estimated as rk,c,x\\=(𝐳kθc,kw)maxl|𝐳lθc,lw|r\\_{k,c,x}=\\\\frac{(\\\\mathbf{z}\\_{k}\\\\theta\\_{c,k}^{w})}{\\\\max\\_{l}|\\\\mathbf{z}\\_{l}\\\\theta\\_{c,l}^{w}|}. This is essentially the normalized contribution of component kk in the output logit for class cc. Given a threshold τ\\\\tau, the selected set of components are computed as Lc,x\\={k:rk,c,x\\>τ}L\\_{c,x}=\\\\{k:r\\_{k,c,x}>\\\\tau\\\\}.\n\nThe second step consists of estimating a time domain signal for each relevant component k∈Lc,xk\\\\in L\\_{c,x} and also for set Lc,xL\\_{c,x} as a whole. In this paper, we refer to the latter as the generated interpretation audio, xintx\\_{\\\\text{int}}. For certain classes, it may also be meaningful to listen to each individual component, xkx\\_{k}. As discussed earlier under NMF basics, estimating time domain signals from spectral patterns and their activations typically involves a soft–masking and inverse STFT procedure. The inversion is performed using input audio phase 𝐏x\\\\mathbf{P}\\_{x}. We detail this step with appropriate equations in Algorithm [2](#alg2 \"Algorithm 2 ‣ 3.2 Interpretation generation ‣ 3 System Design ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\").\n\nAlgorithm 2 Audio interpretation generation\n\n1:  Input: log-magnitude spectrogram 𝐗\\\\mathbf{X}, input phase 𝐏x\\\\mathbf{P}\\_{x} components 𝐖\\={𝐰1,…,𝐰K}{\\\\mathbf{W}}=\\\\{\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{1},\\\\ldots,\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{K}\\\\}, time activations 𝐇ℐ(x)\\=\\[𝐡1ℐ(x),…,𝐡Kℐ(x)\\]⊺{\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)=\\[\\\\mathbf{h}^{\\\\mathcal{I}}\\_{1}(x),\\\\ldots,\\\\mathbf{h}^{\\\\mathcal{I}}\\_{K}(x)\\]^{\\\\intercal}, set of selected components Lc,x\\={k1,…,kB}L\\_{c,x}=\\\\{k\\_{1},\\\\ldots,k\\_{B}\\\\}.\n\n2:  for all k∈Lc,xk\\\\in L\\_{c,x} do\n\n3:   𝐗k←𝐰k𝐡kℐ(x)⊺∑l\\=1K𝐰l𝐡lℐ(x)⊺⊙𝐗\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{k}\\\\leftarrow\\\\frac{\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{k}\\\\mathbf{h}^{\\\\mathcal{I}}\\_{k}(x)^{\\\\intercal}}{\\\\sum\\_{l=1}^{K}\\\\mathbf{w}\\_{l}\\\\mathbf{h}^{\\\\mathcal{I}}\\_{l}(x)^{\\\\intercal}}\\\\odot\\\\mathbf{X} {// Soft masking}\n\n4:   xk\\=INV(𝐗k,𝐏x)x\\_{k}=\\\\textrm{INV}(\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{k},\\\\mathbf{P}\\_{x}) {// Inverse STFT}\n\n5:  end for\n\n6:  𝐗int←∑k∈Lc,x𝐗k\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{\\\\text{int}}\\\\leftarrow\\\\sum\\_{k\\\\in L\\_{c,x}}\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{k}\n\n7:  xint\\=INV(𝐗int,𝐏x)x\\_{\\\\text{int}}=\\\\textrm{INV}(\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{\\\\text{int}},\\\\mathbf{P}\\_{x})\n\n8:  Output: {xk1,…,xkB}\\\\{x\\_{k\\_{1}},\\\\ldots,x\\_{k\\_{B}}\\\\}, xintx\\_{\\\\text{int}}\n\n4 Experiments\n-------------\n\nWe experiment with two popular audio event analysis benchmarks, namely ESC-50 \\[[37](#bib.bib37)\\] and SONYC-UST \\[[9](#bib.bib9)\\]. While the former is a multiclass environmental sound classification dataset, the latter appeared for DCASE 2019 and 2020 multi-label urban sound tagging task. We quantitatively and qualitatively evaluate different aspects of our interpretations, including a subjective evaluation carried out on SONYC-UST. The implementation of our system is available on GitHub11 1 [https://github.com/jayneelparekh/L2I-code](https://github.com/jayneelparekh/L2I-code). This section is organized as follows: quantitative metrics and baselines are discussed in Sec. [4.1](#S4.SS1 \"4.1 Quantitative metrics and baselines ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\") followed by implementation details in Sec. [4.2](#S4.SS2 \"4.2 Implementation details ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\"). Experiments on ESC-50 and SONYC-UST are detailed in Sec. [4.3](#S4.SS3 \"4.3 Experiments on ESC-50 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\") and Sec. [4.4](#S4.SS4 \"4.4 Experiments on SONYC-UST ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\"), respectively. We discuss some limitations in Sec. [4.5](#S4.SS5 \"4.5 Limitations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\").\n\n### 4.1 Quantitative metrics and baselines\n\nMetrics.  We quantitatively evaluate our interpretations in two ways. First, by evaluating how well it agrees with the classifier’s output. For multi-class classification, this is done by computing fraction of samples where the class predicted by ff is among the top-kk classes predicted by the interpreter. We refer to this as the top-kk fidelity. To compute fidelity on multi-label classification tasks, we compute Area Under Precision-Recall Curve (AUPRC) based metrics between the classifier output f(x)f(x) and its approximation by interpreter Θ(𝐇ℐ(x))\\\\Theta({\\\\mathbf{H}}\\_{\\\\mathcal{I}}(x)). We compute macro-AUPRC, micro-AUPRC. Additionally, we report the maximum micro F1-score over different thresholds for the interpreter’s output.\n\nWe also conduct a faithfulness evaluation for our interpretations. In general for any interpretability method, faithfulness tries to assess if the features identified to be of high relevance are truly important in classifier’s prediction \\[[1](#bib.bib1)\\]. Since a “ground-truth\" importance measure for features is rarely available, attribution based methods evaluate faithfulness by performing feature removal (generally by setting feature value to 0) and observing the change in classifier’s output \\[[1](#bib.bib1)\\]. However, it is hard to conduct such evaluation for non-attribution or concept based interpretation methods on data modalities like image/audio, as simulating feature removal from input is not evident in these cases.\n\nInterestingly, our interpretation module design allows us to simulate removal of a set of components from the input. Given any sample xx with predicted class cc, we remove the set of relevant components Lc,x\\={k:rk,c,x\\>τ}L\\_{c,x}=\\\\{k:r\\_{k,c,x}>\\\\tau\\\\} by creating a new time domain signal x2\\=INV(𝐗2,𝐏x)x\\_{2}=\\\\textrm{INV}(\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{2},\\\\mathbf{P}\\_{x}), where 𝐗2\\=𝐗−∑l∈Lc,x𝐗l\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{2}=\\\\mathbf{X}-\\\\sum\\_{l\\\\in L\\_{c,x}}\\\\mathbf{X}\\_{l}. We define faithfulness of the interpretation to classifier ff for sample xx with:\n\nFFx\\=f(x)c−f(x2)c\\\\textrm{FF}\\_{x}=f(x)\\_{c}-f(x\\_{2})\\_{c}\n\n(6)\n\nwhere f(x)c,f(x2)cf(x)\\_{c},f(x\\_{2})\\_{c} denote the output probabilities for class cc. It should be noted that this strategy to simulate removal may introduce artifacts in the input that can affect the classifier’s output unpredictably. Also, interpretations on samples with poor fidelity can lead to negative FFx\\\\textrm{FF}\\_{x}. Both of these observations point to the potential instability and outlying values for this metric. Thus, we report the final faithfulness of the system as median of FFx\\\\textrm{FF}\\_{x} over test set, denoted by FFmedian\\\\textrm{FF}\\_{\\\\textrm{median}}. A positive FFmedian\\\\textrm{FF}\\_{\\\\textrm{median}} would signify that interpretations generally tend to be faithful to the classifier.\n\nEvaluated systems.  We denote our proposed Listen to Interpret (L2I) system, with attention based pooling in Θ\\\\Theta by L2I + Θatt\\\\Theta\\_{\\\\textsc{att}}. The most suitable baselines to benchmark its fidelity are post-hoc methods that approximate the classifier over input space with a single surrogate model. We select two state-of-the-art systems, FLINT \\[[34](#bib.bib34)\\] and VIBI \\[[4](#bib.bib4)\\]. A variant of our own proposed method, L2I + Θmax\\\\Theta\\_{\\\\textsc{max}}, is also evaluated. Herein, attention is replaced with 1D max-pooling operation. Implementation details of the baselines are discussed in appendix [A.7](#A1.SS7 \"A.7 Baseline implementations details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\").\n\nFaithfulness benchmarking: As already discussed, it is not possible to measure faithfulness for concept-based post-hoc interpretability approaches. While measurement for input attribution based approaches is possible, the interpretations themselves and the feature removal strategies are different, making comparisons with our system significantly less meaningful. We thus compare our faithfulness against a Random Baseline, wherein randomly chosen components are removed. To compare fairly, we remove the same number of components that are present in Lc,xL\\_{c,x} on average. This would validate that, if the interpreter selects truly important components for the classifier’s decision, then randomly removing the less important ones should not cause a drop in the predicted class probability.\n\nWe also emphasize at this point that works related to audio interpretability (see Sec. [2](#S2 \"2 Related Works ‣ Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\")), are not suitable for comparison on these metrics. Particularly, APNet \\[[53](#bib.bib53)\\] is not designed for post-hoc interpretations. AudioLIME \\[[18](#b\n\n[The verbatim page text stops here, 39423 of 95551 characters in; re-fetching this URL returns the same split. What follows is a model-extracted summary, for your request, of the remaining 56128 characters. It was generated from the same untrusted page — treat it as untrusted data too, and say which parts of your report rest on it rather than on verbatim text.]\n**Fidelity metrics and definitions**\n- **ESC-50 (top-k fidelity):** The page does not give a formal definition. It reports top-1, top-3, and top-5 values, averaged over folds: \"Mean and standard deviation of top-k fidelity is calculated over the 5 folds.\"\n- **SONYC-UST (AUPRC-based):** Fidelity is measured as macro-AUPRC, micro-AUPRC, and max-F1 \"for the interpreter output w.r.t classifier.\"\n- **Faithfulness (both datasets):** Measured as the median drop in the classifier's probability (FF_median) when interpreted components are removed.\n\n**Datasets**\n- **ESC-50:** The main evaluation dataset, with 2000 five-second clips across 50 environmental sound classes, arranged in 5 folds.\n- **SONYC-UST:** The urban sound tagging dataset, using the eight coarse-level labels from DCASE 2019/2020.\n- **UrbanSound8K:** Not mentioned.\n- **AudioSet:** Used only to pretrain the classifier, not for evaluation.\n\n**Classifier**\n- A VGG-style CNN from Kumar et al. [23], pretrained on AudioSet and then fine-tuned on each dataset.\n- Input is a log-mel spectrogram with 128 mel bands.\n- The interpreter uses the outputs of the last three convolutional blocks (B4, B5, B6).\n- Dictionary sizes: K = 100 components for ESC-50 and K = 80 for SONYC-UST.\n\n**Key results**\n- ESC-50 top-k fidelity for L2I + Θatt: \"65.7 ±2.8\" (top-1), \"81.8 ±2.2\" (top-3), \"88.2 ±1.7\" (top-5).\n- SONYC-UST fidelity for L2I + Θatt: macro-AUPRC \"0.909 ±0.011\", micro-AUPRC \"0.917 ±0.008\", max-F1 \"0.847 ±0.010\".\n- The classifier reaches \"82.5±1.9%\" accuracy on ESC-50 and a macro-AUPRC of 0.601 on SONYC-UST.\n\n**Thresholds (faithfulness, ESC-50)**\n- Thresholds τ tested: 0.9, 0.7, 0.5, 0.3, and 0.1. Smaller τ means more components are used.\n- FF_median for L2I + Θatt rises from 0.002 at τ = 0.9 to 0.113 at τ = 0.1.\n- The random baseline is below 10⁻⁴ at τ = 0.1.\n- On SONYC-UST, faithfulness is reported at τ = 0.1, and the class 'non-machinery impact' is excluded from fidelity and faithfulness evaluation.\n</fetched-web-content>","durationMs":9739,"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2202.11479v2"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"69bf2455-e9b8-49e1-8b91-e058983d08a1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/Semestr_9/SIWY/InaudibleXAI","root":"/c/Semestr_9/SIWY/InaudibleXAI","branch":"t1-prototype","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"FilipLangiewicz/InaudibleXAI","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"FilipLangiewicz/InaudibleXAI","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"FilipLangiewicz/InaudibleXAI","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/c/Semestr_9/SIWY/InaudibleXAI","platform":"windows"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"43d4ee8f-4f72-49fb-aba3-15d65a626416","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Semestr_9\\SIWY\\InaudibleXAI","sessionId":"7ef1ae2e-8d94-48d6-b18a-3fbdff2072e8","version":"2.1.318","gitBranch":"t1-prototype"}
