{"parentUuid":"8b2befb8-0ba2-4fb3-aa3c-2e81ec03d4e1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a62d817979fb0ca2b","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkZivrMzG7TSTSunMJLo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01No4NiZHq7ydDAxd9BRts3w","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Report on three sources. Verbatim vs. summary-only parts are marked. Quotes are kept under 125 characters.\n\nSource access\n- A) The arxiv.org/pdf/2403.13086 fetch returned raw PDF binary, which the harness saved locally, so I did not use it. I read the HTML version instead: https://arxiv.org/html/2403.13086 (v3). Its first ~39k characters are verbatim. The rest was model-summarized. The summary's claim that the excerpt \"does not define\" the metrics is wrong, because the definitions are in the verbatim section 3.1.\n- B) https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11479 (abstract page only) and https://arxiv.org/html/2202.11479v2 (v2 full text). The first ~39k of 95k characters are verbatim (through Sec. 4.1). The rest is summarized.\n- C) The journals.sagepub.com URL returned HTTP 403 (permanent; retrying will fail the same way). The NCBI URL returned 301 to https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5502906/, which is the same article on NCBI's new domain, and I read that. About 39k of 69k characters are verbatim. The rest is summarized.\n\nA) Paissan, Ravanelli, Subakan, \"Listenable Maps for Audio Classifiers\" (L-MAC), ICML 2024\n- Faithfulness metrics (verbatim definitions, Sec. 3.1). Each is computed per example and averaged over N.\n  - FF (Faithfulness on Spectra), from Parekh et al. 2022: FF_n = f(X_n)_c − f(X_n ⊙ (1−M)). The text says \"Larger is better.\"\n  - AI (Average Increase, from Chattopadhay et al. 2018): AI = (1/N) Σ 1[f(X_n ⊙ M)_c > f(X_n)_c] · 100. Larger is better.\n  - AD (Average Drop): AD = (1/N) Σ max(0, f(X_n)_c − f(X_n ⊙ M)_c) / f(X_n)_c · 100. Smaller is better.\n  - AG (Average Gain, from Zhang et al. 2023): AG = (1/N) Σ max(0, f(X_n ⊙ M)_c − f(X_n)_c) / (1 − f(X_n)_c) · 100. Larger is better.\n  - Fid-In (from Paissan et al. 2023): the fraction of examples where argmax f(X_n) equals argmax f(X_n ⊙ M). Larger is better.\n  - SPS (Sparseness, from Chalasani et al. 2020): computed with the Quantus library. Larger means sparser.\n  - COMP (Complexity, from Bhatt et al. 2020): entropy of the feature-contribution distribution, computed with Quantus. Smaller is better.\n  - \"M\" here is the decoder's mask M_θ(h), applied to the linear spectrogram X.\n- Thresholds: no faithfulness threshold is stated. The only thresholds are training hyperparameters. The \"CCT\" (cross-correlation threshold) of 0.6 or 0.7 is applied during fine-tuning to decide whether a mask is similar enough to the target. The baseline L2I uses relevance thresholds RT = 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8.\n- Audibility: no objective audibility check is described. The text says \"masking in the STFT domain gives the ability to produce listenable interpretations.\" The only perceptual evaluation is a user study: 15 participants rated the interpretations 0–100 using webMUSHRA. Instructions included \"pay attention to audio quality also.\" Table 3 (verbatim): MOS-1 is L-MAC 59.13, FT λg=16 59.8, FT λg=16 CCT0.7 63.7, L2I 55.1. MOS-2 is L-MAC 64.00, FT 66.7, CCT0.7 67.2, L2I 50.8.\n- Dataset and classifier (verbatim): ESC-50 is the main dataset, with ESC-50 plus WHAM! noise for training. UrbanSound8K is in Appendix B. The classifier is CNN14 (Kong et al. 2020). It was trained on folds 1, 2, 3 and reaches \"75% and 78%\" accuracy on folds 5 and 4. It was pretrained on VGGSound using SimCLR. The L-MAC decoder is frozen-classifier based. In-domain uses ESC-50 folds 4 and 5 with WHAM! noise. Out-of-domain uses mixtures of folds 4 and 5.\n- Key numbers, Table 1 in-domain, STFT-Mel (verbatim): L-MAC AI 36.25, AD 1.15, AG 23.50, FF 0.20, Fid-In 0.42, SPS 0.47, COMP 4.71. GradCAM AI 8.50, AD 10.11, AG 1.47, FF 0.17, Fid-In 0.33. Saliency AD 15.79. Table 2 out-of-domain, L-MAC: AI 60.63, AD 4.82, AG 35.85, FF 0.39, Fid-In 0.81.\n- Ambiguity: the text does not clearly say whether metrics are computed on the ISTFT-resynthesized waveform or on the masked spectrogram fed to the classifier.\n- The Appendix B and Table 4 white/LJSpeech noise results come from the summary only. I did not verify them.\n\nB) Parekh et al., \"Listen to Interpret: Post-hoc Interpretability for Audio Networks with NMF\" (L2I), NeurIPS 2022\n- Fidelity (verbatim, Sec. 4.1): top-k fidelity is \"the fraction of samples where the class predicted by f is among the top-k classes predicted by the interpreter.\" For multi-label SONYC-UST, the metrics are macro-AUPRC, micro-AUPRC, and maximum micro F1.\n- Faithfulness (verbatim): components whose relevance r_{k,c,x} > τ are removed by building x2 = INV(X − Σ X_l, P_x). FF_x = f(x)_c − f(x2)_c. The reported value is FF_median over the test set, because the removal \"may introduce artifacts\" and can make FF_x unstable and negative. A random baseline removes the same number of randomly chosen components.\n- Relevance threshold τ (verbatim): component k is selected if r_{k,c,x} > τ. Thresholds of 0.9, 0.7, 0.5, 0.3, 0.1, and the FF_median values (0.002 to 0.113), come from the summary of the unread part. I did not verify them.\n- Datasets (verbatim): ESC-50 and SONYC-UST. ESC-50 has 2000 five-second clips in 50 classes, in 5 folds (summary). Reported ESC-50 top-1/3/5 fidelity of 65.7 ±2.8, 81.8 ±2.2, 88.2 ±1.7 is from the summary. Verbatim-supported: the paper mentions \"a subjective evaluation carried out on SONYC-UST\" but I did not read its details.\n- Classifier (summary only): a VGG-style CNN from Kumar et al. [23], pretrained on AudioSet and fine-tuned per dataset, using 128-band log-mel input. It is not CNN14. Dictionary size K=100 for ESC-50 and K=80 for SONYC-UST.\n- Audibility: the listenable output is produced by ISTFT with the input phase (verbatim). I did not find an objective audibility check. The subjective evaluation is on SONYC-UST, with details in the unread part.\n- The abstract is verbatim: \"interpret decisions of a trained network in terms of high-level audio objects that are also listenable for the end-user.\"\n\nC) Lakens 2017, \"Equivalence Tests: A Practical Primer for t Tests, Correlations, and Meta-Analyses,\" Soc Psychol Personal Sci 8(4):355–362 (PMC5502906)\n- TOST (verbatim): \"two composite null hypotheses are tested: H01: Δ ≤ −ΔL and H02: Δ ≥ ΔU. When both these one-sided tests can be statistically rejected, we can conclude that −ΔL < Δ < ΔU.\" The t-statistics are t_L = (M1 − M2 − ΔL)/SE and t_U = (M1 − M2 − ΔU)/SE. Both one-sided tests must reject.\n- Bounds (abstract, verbatim): \"an upper and lower equivalence bound is specified based on the smallest effect size of interest.\" Typical example: d = −.3 to .3.\n- Choosing bounds (verbatim): \"researchers set the bounds to the smallest effect size they have sufficient power to detect\" (the paper's proposal when no theoretical or practical bounds exist). The paper says the SESOI is determined by feasibility. Its example: with n=100 per group, 80% power, α=.05, the sensitivity-analysis SESOI is Δ=0.389, and the equivalence bounds are ±0.414.\n- Justifications (verbatim, from the first ~39k characters). The paper says theories often have \"no specific theoretical limitations\" and that cost-benefit boundaries are absent, so feasibility is the main determinant. It says \"the maximum sample size you are willing to collect implicitly determines your SESOI.\" Benchmarks (small/medium/large) are \"typically recommended as a last resort.\" Previous studies: Simonsohn (2015) suggests setting the bound to the effect size the original study had 33% power to detect. Prespecification follows CONSORT for RCTs. The paper says the best solution is to specify the SESOI \"when they publish an original result.\" It discusses online studies with no feasibility limit, where benchmarks are a starting point.\n- Measurement error: I did not find this as a justification in the verbatim text. The unread part was summarized, and the summary also did not mention it. This remains unverified.\n- Correction to the summary: the summary said \"smallest effect size of interest\" does not appear in the text. That is wrong, because it appears in the abstract and the TOST section.\n- 90% CI rule (verbatim): \"Readers are free to replace decisions based on p values by decisions based on 90% CIs if they wish.\" Equivalence is concluded when \"the 90% CI around the observed mean difference should exclude the Δ_L and Δ_U values.\" For meta-analysis: \"If the 90% CI falls within the equivalence bounds, the observed meta-analytic effect is statistically equivalent.\" The rationale that a 90% CI matches two one-sided 5% tests (1 − 2α) comes from the summary only.\n- Worked example (verbatim): Moery and Calin-Jageman's replication sets the bound to d = .48, which equals .384 scale points. Results: t_L = 3.14, t_U = −2.69, p = .004. \"We can reject effects larger than .384 scale points.\"\n\nRelevance to the project: L-MAC's faithfulness metrics (AI, AD, AG, FF, Fid-In) are all confidence-drop or confidence-gain based. The paper sets no faithfulness threshold. The Lakens sources describe equivalence bounds as a function of SESOI or feasibility. Neither L-MAC nor L2I objectively checks audibility of the audio explanation."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17616,"cache_read_input_tokens":77819,"output_tokens":8563,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4664},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17616},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8563,"cache_read_input_tokens":77819,"cache_creation_i